            <ArticleTitle>Diet-Microbiome Interactions in Health Are Controlled by Intestinal Nitrogen Source Constraints.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>140-151</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1550-4131(16)30553-8</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.cmet.2016.10.021</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Diet influences health and patterns of disease in populations. How different diets do this and why outcomes of diets vary between individuals are complex and involve interaction with the gut microbiome. A major challenge for predicting health outcomes of the host-microbiome dynamic is reconciling the effects of different aspects of diet (food composition or intake rate) on the system. Here we show that microbial community assembly is fundamentally shaped by a dichotomy in bacterial strategies to access nitrogen in the gut environment. Consequently, the pattern of dietary protein intake constrains the host-microbiome dynamic in ways that are common to a very broad range of diet manipulation strategies. These insights offer a mechanism for the impact of high protein intake on metabolic health and form the basis for a general theory of the impact of different diet strategies on host-microbiome outcomes.</AbstractText>
